WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.190
Standards you can keep, dignity for every customer, and a simple care notebook.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.750
This is the video for module three of Customer Care and Respectful Communication.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.780
Good intentions fade on busy days. So in this module, you turn customer care into things you can check.

4
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.870
You will serve every customer with dignity, including those who are easy to overlook.

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.940
You will protect their information.

6
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.430
And you will keep a simple notebook that shows whether your care is improving.

7
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:42.750
A service standard is a promise about how you treat every customer, written so you can check it. We give good service cannot be checked.

8
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.240
We greet every customer within 10 seconds can be checked on any day.

9
00:00:48.590 --> 00:01:01.710
Choose numbers you can keep on your busiest day, not your quietest. An honest 1-hour reply promise you always keep beats a 10-minute promise you break every market day. Five to seven standards are enough.

10
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:14.820
For example, Bisi runs a POS stand in Ilorin. Her customers complained about surprise charges, long waits when the network was slow, and confusion when transfers failed.

11
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:30.030
So she wrote: I say the charge before every transaction. If the network is down, I tell each waiting customer within 1 minute. If a transaction fails, I give the reference in writing and check with you the next day.

12
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:40.900
She added two more: greet every customer, and serve strictly in order of arrival, with priority for older customers and anyone with a disability.

13
00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:48.890
After one week, she had announced 5 of 6 network failures within a minute. She circled the miss and wrote why.

14
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:57.610
Bank reversals are handled by the bank, not by Bisi. So she promises what she will do: tell, check and follow up.

15
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:10.780
Respect is tested most with customers who are easy to overlook. Rule one: ask before you help. Would you like me to read the price list for you? Never grab a person's arm or wheelchair.

16
00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.790
Rule two: speak to the customer, not their companion. They are the one buying.

17
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.600
Give an older customer a seat and a slower pace, with no rushing at the till.

18
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:29.810
Face a deaf customer in good light, speak clearly without shouting, and write or type on your phone.

19
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:42.980
For someone who cannot read, read terms aloud in private, and never comment on their reading. Give the same prices and patience to everyone, whatever their tribe, religion or clothes.

20
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.150
Hauwa greets Emeka by name and says who she is.

21
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:57.560
He wants 20,000 naira. She asks before guiding his hand to the keypad, and turns away while he enters his PIN. She never asks for it.

22
00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:02.430
She tells him the charge, 200 naira, before starting.

23
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:17.040
She pays out only in 1,000 naira notes, counting aloud into his hand, 20 notes in all. Then she reads the receipt amount and reference aloud. He leaves knowing exactly what he received and paid.

24
00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:33.990
The Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 governs personal data. For you, that means: collect only what you need. Hide names and numbers before sharing any screenshot. Do not add customers to groups they did not agree to.

25
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:42.300
Never discuss one customer's debts with another. Never ask for a PIN or code. And keep your notebook and phone locked.

26
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:54.600
You cannot improve what you only feel. An exercise book is enough. Write each entry straight after the conversation, first names only, and be honest in the kept column.

27
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:03.680
For example, Amaka sells dresses in Owerri. She made 40 promises and kept 34. That is 85 percent.

28
00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:10.090
She had 8 complaints and resolved 6 the same day. That is 75 percent.

29
00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:17.200
Late delivery caused 4 of the 8 complaints, and all 6 broken promises were delivery dates.

30
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:30.740
So she chose one action: add two extra days to every quoted date, and message customers the moment a job runs late. With small numbers, look at the counts too, and compare month to month.

31
00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:38.050
To remember. Write standards you can check and keep. Ask before you help. Treat everyone the same.

32
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.540
Protect customer information. And count your notebook each month, then fix one thing.

33
00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:49.510
Now write and test your standards, do a dignity check, and start your care notebook.

34
00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.800
Continue with the lessons below.
